course: euphemisms
lesson: 16
title: Soft words for hard things: school reports and teachers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Teachers write reports that parents read, so they choose kind, careful words instead of blunt ones.
explain: A report rarely says "lazy" or "bossy"; it says "capable of more" or "a natural leader", and the parent must read between the lines.
explain: The soft words are there to be polite and to keep the child hopeful, but they still carry a real message.
explain: For example, "Tom has his own way of doing things" usually means Tom does not follow the teacher's instructions.
explain: Some school words are official jargon, like "approaching standard" in the US or "requires improvement" for a UK school.
fact: "Approaching standard" (US school jargon) means the pupil is not yet meeting the required level.
fact: "Needs encouragement" on a report usually means the pupil is lazy or unmotivated (does not want to try).
fact: "Capable of more" means the pupil is underachieving: doing less well than he or she could.
fact: "Could do better" (an everyday UK phrase) means the pupil is not working hard enough.
fact: "Easily distracted" means the pupil does not pay attention.
fact: "Finds it hard to stay on task" means the pupil wastes time in class.
fact: "Has his own way of doing things" means he does not follow instructions.
fact: "Confident speaker" on a report can mean the pupil talks over other children.
fact: "Creative spelling" is a joking way to say poor spelling.
fact: "Natural leader" can be spin for bossy; spin means choosing nice-sounding words to make something bad sound good.
fact: "Knows his own mind" is a kind way to say stubborn.
fact: "Enthusiastic contributor" can mean the pupil shouts out answers without waiting.
fact: "Would benefit from" (more practice, more reading) means the pupil badly needs it.
fact: "Has made some progress" often means very little progress.
fact: "Working at his own pace" means he is very slow.
fact: "Not working to her full potential" is a polite way to say she is lazy.
fact: "A challenging class" means a badly behaved class.
fact: "Additional needs" (official UK term) means special educational needs or a disability.
fact: "A learning opportunity" is spin for a failure or a mistake.
fact: "Requires improvement" is an official UK inspection grade meaning the school is poor.
exercise: A US report card says a pupil is "approaching standard" in maths. What does it mean? | Not yet at the required level | Nearly top of the class | Walking towards the standard | Doing better than expected
exercise: What does "needs encouragement" usually mean on a school report? | The pupil is lazy or unmotivated | The pupil needs more praise for great work | The pupil is very shy but hard-working | The pupil needs a cheerleader at sports day
exercise: A teacher writes "Sam is capable of more." What is she really saying? | Sam is underachieving | Sam can lift heavy things | Sam is the best in the class | Sam should skip a year
exercise: What does "could do better" mean on a British report? | The pupil is not working hard enough | The pupil is already doing very well | The pupil could win a prize | The pupil does better than others
exercise: What does "easily distracted" mean on a report? | The pupil does not pay attention | The pupil is easily upset | The pupil is quick to learn | The pupil is easy to teach
exercise: What does "finds it hard to stay on task" mean? | The pupil wastes time in class | The pupil cannot find the task sheet | The pupil works too fast | The pupil is often absent
exercise: What does a teacher mean by "Leo has his own way of doing things"? | Leo does not follow instructions | Leo invents clever new methods | Leo works best alone at home | Leo is very independent and tidy
exercise: A report calls a girl "a confident speaker". What can this hide? | She talks over other children | She is the best singer in school | She never speaks in class | She speaks two languages
exercise: What does "creative spelling" really mean? | Poor spelling | Spelling in fancy handwriting | Inventing beautiful new words | Spelling better than the teacher
exercise: A report says a boy is "a natural leader". What can it be spin for? | Bossy | Class captain by vote | Good at running races | Always at the front of the line
exercise: What does "knows his own mind" mean about a pupil? | He is stubborn | He remembers everything | He is good at thinking games | He understands the brain
exercise: What does "enthusiastic contributor" often mean? | Shouts out answers without waiting | Gives money to the school | Brings snacks for the class | Puts the most work into projects
exercise: "She would benefit from more reading at home." What does this mean? | She badly needs more reading | She would earn money by reading | She reads more than enough | Reading at home is optional for her
exercise: What does "has made some progress" often mean on a report? | Very little progress | Excellent progress | Progress in every subject | Moved up a group
exercise: What does "working at his own pace" mean? | He is very slow | He walks fast in the corridor | He chooses his own subjects | He works faster than the others
exercise: What does "not working to her full potential" mean? | She is lazy | She is ill and tired | She is working too hard | She is the top pupil
exercise: A teacher says "I have a challenging class this year." What does she mean? | A badly behaved class | A class doing hard maths | A class that loves challenges | A very clever class
exercise: In UK schools, what does "additional needs" mean? | Special educational needs or a disability | Needing extra pencils | Wanting a second lunch | Needing more homework
exercise: The teacher calls a failed test "a learning opportunity". What happened? | The pupil failed or made a mistake | The pupil learned a lot and passed | The test was a bonus lesson | The pupil was given a reward
exercise: A school inspector rates a UK school "requires improvement". What does it mean? | The school is poor | The school needs new paint | The school is outstanding | The school is being built
test: Mum reads: "Ella is a confident speaker and an enthusiastic contributor." What is Ella probably doing in class? | Talking over others and shouting out answers | Sitting quietly and listening | Giving speeches to the whole school | Collecting money for charity
test: "ugh my report says i'm working at my own pace lol" — what did the teacher mean? | You are very slow | You are a fast runner | You set your own homework | You are ahead of everyone
test: Dad: "Creative spelling, it says. Creative! Bloody hell, mate." Why is Dad cross? | The teacher means the spelling is poor | The teacher praised the spelling | The child won an art prize | The child forgot the report
test: Gran says: "Your teacher thinks you know your own mind, eh? Like your grandad." What is she hinting? | That you are stubborn | That you are clever at quizzes | That you remember names well | That you look like grandad
test: A news line: "Local school rated 'requires improvement' by inspectors." Is this good news for the school? | No, it means the school is rated poor | Yes, it means the school is the best | Yes, it means new buildings are coming | No, it means the school will close tomorrow
test: Mia's brother reckons "capable of more" is a compliment. Right? | No, it means she is underachieving | Yes, it means she is the strongest | Yes, it means she can do everything | No, it means she is ill
test: The head says at assembly: "Year 5 have been a challenging class this term." What happened? | Year 5 behaved badly | Year 5 did the hardest work | Year 5 won a competition | Year 5 asked lots of good questions
test: "she says he 'finds it hard to stay on task' — what task, he never even starts" — what is the report saying? | He wastes time in class | He cannot find his classroom | He has too many tasks | He finishes too early
test: A teacher tells a parent, "Josh has made some progress in reading." The parent frowns. Why? | It often means very little progress | It means Josh is top of the class | It means Josh skipped reading | It means Josh needs glasses
test: A friend gets a failed spelling test back. The teacher says, "Think of it as a learning opportunity!" What really happened? | He failed the test | He got full marks | He got a free lesson | He was chosen to teach
test: "report says i would benefit from more practice with times tables, so like optional right" — is it optional? | No, it means you badly need it | Yes, it is just a suggestion | Yes, only if you want a prize | No, it means you must teach it
test: A report says "Kai has his own way of doing things, which is not always the way he was asked." What is Kai doing? | Not following instructions | Inventing a new subject | Helping the teacher mark | Working at home instead
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a pupil described in a written report as "not working to her full potential"? | The pupil is insufficiently diligent | The pupil has exceeded all expectations | The pupil is unwell and absent | The pupil has attained the maximum standard
exam: Identify the principal meaning conveyed when an educational institution in the United Kingdom is graded "requires improvement" pursuant to inspection. | The institution is of poor quality | The institution's premises require repair | The institution is of outstanding quality | The institution is to be expanded
exam: It is stated that a pupil is "easily distracted". Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of this expression. | The pupil fails to pay attention | The pupil is readily upset | The pupil acquires knowledge rapidly | The pupil is easily instructed
exam: Whereas a pupil is reported to be "approaching standard" in the United States, determine what is in fact being communicated. | The pupil has not yet reached the required level | The pupil has exceeded the required level | The pupil is physically nearing a building | The pupil has been awarded a distinction
